{
    "key": "level-02-too-much-powder",
    "name": "Too Much Powder",
    "notes": "The campaign baseline; levels 1, 3, 4 and Sandbox are this file with the ladder cells changed and nothing else. Numbers from docs/03 (cycles, curves, shelves), docs/04 (windows), docs/13 (groove, orders), docs/16 (hazards), docs/17 (carry), docs/18 (day, grading, economy) and docs/19 (the one-line breakpoint). The level exists to make somebody say 'Stop making powder.': a keg is two pours at 2.8 s while a stick eats half a keg every 6.0 s, so Powder outruns Assembly's demand more than four to one and the floor ring fills while the jig starves. orderBook is 10, not docs/18's 11 - with firstAt 20 and everySeconds 60 an eleventh truck would arrive at 620 s, past the 600 s whistle. qtyPerTruck is 7 on a 0.25 ramp, not docs/13's 8 on 0.5: a stick reaches the truck in one pair of hands at a time, so a book is a labour sum over the whole crew and not the jig's output, and 8 on a 0.5 ramp asked ten trucks for 100 sticks that six people cannot carry across this floor inside 600 s. The maxPlayers 3 breakpoint hands a crew at the floor of the range a shorter book on a smaller truck (ADR 0013) - three people have one spare pair of hands, not three. From this level on the explosion is a hard loss. F14 retunes the crimp to a 0.4 s lead-in, 0.4 s clicks and a 180 ms OK window: at 0.2 s the first click landed 200 ms after the knot the player was still resolving, the whole ratchet was over in 600 ms, and the +/-200 ms window around the third click opened exactly on the second, so tapping the wrong click scored OK. The new numbers clear the tempoFloorsMs mark floor (400 >= 250), the coupling rule (180 < 200) and the lead floor (400 >= 400), and put every marker in A's phrase on the same 400 ms beat as the knots. The 0.4 s ratchet takes A's phrase from 4.2 s to 4.8 s, so assembly-a's nominalSeconds 6.0 is now exactly the flawless worst case - dud keg, long fuse, every mark clean - rather than an expectation with slack. Nothing is mistuned by it: the Jig supplies its own state and progress, so the cycle-rule fallback that reads nominalSeconds never runs here, and a pair stays machine-bound at 5.0 s. Raise it to ~6.8 only as a deliberate F12 balance pass.",
